---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="Ravichandra — platform engineer across AWS, Azure, and GCP. Field notes on GitOps, Kubernetes, and IaC at cloud-dispatch."
  path="/about/"
>
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>About</h1>
    <p class="meta">Author · cloud-dispatch</p>
    <p>
      I’m <strong>Ravichandra</strong>, a platform engineer working across
      <strong>AWS</strong>, <strong>Azure</strong>, and <strong>GCP</strong> with a
      focus on GitOps, Kubernetes, Infrastructure as Code, and platform engineering.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>cloud-dispatch</em> is where I document architecture decisions, implementation
      details, and operational lessons from building production infrastructure. I write
      about practical engineering over hype—what worked, what failed, and the trade-offs
      behind each decision.
    </p>
    <p>
      Recent series: a <strong>GitHub-only IDP</strong> built with IssueOps and OpenTofu
      (control plane vs workload trust, public-demo gates, provision path, bootstrap).
      Sibling notes cover a Kubernetes / Backstage IDP and Vault Hub — different planes,
      same habit of writing the seams down.
    </p>
    <p>
      Start with the <a href={`${base}posts/`}>notes</a> if you want the field write-ups —
      the IssueOps arc begins with
      <a href={`${base}posts/github-idp-issueops-part-1/`}>Why control and workload must live apart</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      GitHub:
      <a
        href="https://github.com/ravichandrapatel"
        rel="me noopener noreferrer"
        >github.com/ravichandrapatel</a
      >
    </p>
    <p>
      LinkedIn:
      <a
        href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/ravichandra-ch"
        rel="me noopener noreferrer"
        >linkedin.com/in/ravichandra-ch</a
      >
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
